      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Journalism often employs different frames based on the demographics and perceived nature of the protest. Protests by suburbanites against policy like a tax law might be described using neutral or even laudatory language, focusing on \"civility\" or \"economic concerns.\" Conversely, protests involving minority youth protesting police conduct are more likely to receive terminology emphasizing disruption, implying \"unrest,\" \"riots,\" or focusing on confrontation. These linguistic choices subtly shape the public perception of who is protesting and why.",
